<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brora</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brora is a village on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutherland">Sutherland</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands">Scottish Highlands</a>, at the mouth of the River Brora. The surrounding valley, Strath Brora, is rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> brochs. From the 16th century Brora had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mine, unusual in the Highlands, and it later had a woollen mill and the Clynelish whisky distillery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brora.jpg" alt="Brora" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stanley Howe · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harbour_Road,_Brora_-_geograph.org.uk_-_486882.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Brora, Brùra in Scottish Gaelic, takes its name from Old Norse words meaning river with a bridge. The village stands where the A9 road and the Far North Line cross the River Brora, and it has its own railway station. The white sandstone of the nearby Clynelish quarry belongs to the Brora Formation of the Middle to Late Jurassic, and stone from the quarry was used to build London Bridge, Liverpool Cathedral and Dunrobin Castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Brora grew into a small industrial village. At various times it had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> pit, the most northerly coal mine in the UK, as well as boat building, salt pans, fish curing, a lemonade factory, a wool mill, brickworks and a stone quarry, and two distilleries, the new Clynelish Distillery and the old Clynelish distillery, now called the Brora distillery. Thanks to its wool industry it was the first place in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> to have electricity, which earned it the local nickname Electric City.</p><p>Members of the Clyne Heritage Society, recording structures eroding from the dunes of the Back Beach in 2005, found a map of 1812 marking Old Salt Pans at the spot and records showing that the pans had been built in 1598 and abandoned within a few years. Like many smaller fishing ports, Brora lost much of its fishing in the years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>: in 1906 the Fishery Board reported that it was becoming more popular as a summer resort and declining as a fishing port, its fishermen working ashore as labourers for much of the year. From 1940 to 1986 the Brora Y Station, south-east of the village, served as a government listening station.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A baronial-style clock tower in the village serves as a war memorial. Brora Primary School occupies the building of the former Brora High School, whose secondary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> closed in 1985, and older children attend Golspie High School. Brora Rangers F.C., founded in 1879, have played in the Highland Football League since 1962, and the village has an 18-hole links golf course laid out by James Braid in 1923.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brora” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lugi are known only from Ptolemy's Geography of about 150, which places them in the far north of Scotland, usually located on the east coast of Sutherland around the Dornoch Firth and the <b>Brora</b> area, between the Decantae and the Cornavii (Caithness).</blockquote><a href="/state/lugi">Lugi</a></li></ul></section>
